  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 413 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThere are only so many different ways you can talk about the current \nand prolonged heat across the area. So, keeping the take home point \nsimple ... the heat continues for at least another few days for a \ngood portion of the area. \n\nSynoptically speaking, not much change from cycle to cycle among the \ndeterministic or ensemble guidance. Pattern evolution over the next \ncouple/few days remains well signaled among the flagship \ndeterministic models and their accompanying ensembles. This takes \nthe existing Omega-block look to the pattern and really just squats \nit over the CONUS into early next week. The more subtle evolution \nwill see the central ridge axis  drift/retrograde westward and \namplify as the eastern cutoff low churns down into/around New \nEngland and the PNW cutoff struggles to make any headway. Aside from \nthe continued heat, this too continues to keep any appreciable \nprecipitation chances primarily north of the area through the \nweekend. Expect conditions through the weekend tending to be repeats \nof the last couple/few days with highs ranging from the mid 90s to \naround 100 and heat index values a few degrees higher up to around \n105. All of this is bolstered by very tight ensemble temperature \nspreads seen across various visualization tools. No changes to the \nfirst portion of the Extreme Heat Warning given the above. \n\nBy Sunday afternoon, there remains signaled some relief for N/NE \nportions of the area in response to weak mid-level height falls \n(ridge axis drifts ever so slightly further west) and a backdoor \ncold front as a large surface high drops into the Great Lakes \nregion. This pushes highs in those areas down into the low to mid \n90s. Unfortunately, relief quickly peters out and leaves areas S and \nW of a line from around Maryville MO to Boonville MO left \nbaking in heat. Ensemble guidance remains quite confident in \nthat heat with NBM probabilities >70-90 percent for highs of 95 \ndeg F or greater for locations like St. Joseph, Kansas City and \nprobabilities of 60 percent or greater as far east of \nBrookfield, Moberly, and Columbia. With a similar story playing \nout Monday, this helps allow N/NE areas of the Extreme Heat \nWarning to expire Saturday.\n\nMonday/Tuesday, the mid-upper level pattern becomes more \nprogressive, with the western CONUS cutoff low flattening the ridge \nas it moves across the Northern and Canadian Plains. Before it is \nable to possibly bring about appreciable temperature relief, later \nin the week, Tuesday may be the hottest day of the heat wave as it \npushes 850mb thermal ridge axis over the area. A handful of 100s may \nbe possible. This too results in these areas remaining in an Extreme \nHeat Warning through Tuesday evening. Ensemble temperature spreads \nincrease substantially mid-late week in response to uncertainty in \njust how far south cold front is able to make it. \n\nThroughout this time period, appreciable precipitation will be \nabundantly hard to come by. The best opportunities for any \nprecipitation will tend to be over northern and eastern areas as \nshortwaves/disturbances traverse the ridge.",
